// ---------- Discussion ----------
children.push(
  h1(T`Discussion`),
  h2(T`Principal findings`),
  mix([
    T`In this two-sample and multivariable Mendelian randomization study using the largest available European-ancestry AF GWAS, genetically proxied consumption of sweetened beverages was not causally associated with atrial fibrillation. This null finding was consistent across five conceptually distinct exposures—including sugar added to tea or coffee, the closest genetic proxy for milk-tea-type beverages—across three European outcome sources and all complementary estimators. Three nominal IVW signals emerged, but none survived scrutiny: the inverse association for sugar added to coffee failed to replicate in the nearly twice-larger primary outcome; a nominal inverse association for sugar added to tea in FinnGen R13 was absent from the other three sources and attenuated in the meta-analysis; and the positive association for coffee intake, although nominally significant after Benjamini–Hochberg correction (meta-analytic OR 1.34; P = 0.002), failed every pre-specified consistency criterion. East Asian replication estimates were imprecise but directionally consistent. The positive lifestyle control was directionally associated with AF in all four cohorts and significant in the European meta-analysis, confirming analytical sensitivity.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Comparison with previous work`),
  mix([
    T`Our findings diverge from the two UK Biobank cohort analyses that reported 10–20% higher incident AF risk among high consumers of SSBs or ASBs [2,3]. A previous smaller MR study of SSB and pure-fruit-juice intake reported a positive causal association between SSBs and AF (OR 1.023, 1.007–1.038; P = 0.0039) [26]. That analysis used an AF outcome GWAS with 3,818 cases—one-sixteenth the size of ours—selected instruments at a relaxed threshold (P < 10⁻⁵) and did not model adiposity; the reported effect lies within our confidence bounds, and our multivariable, colocalization and CAUSE analyses argue against a causal interpretation. Three considerations help reconcile this divergence. First, the observational hazard ratios were modest and are compatible with residual confounding: sweetened-beverage consumers differ systematically from non-consumers in adiposity, diet quality and lifestyle, and our multivariable and colocalization results demonstrate directly that adiposity-related pathways account for the only nominal genetic signal we observed. Second, MR estimates reflect lifelong average exposure, whereas cohorts capture mid-life intake over about a decade; if a true effect requires cumulative exposure beyond instrumented intake, MR would be biased towards the null. Third, our confidence bounds exclude causal effects larger than ~9–20% relative increase per intake unit for most exposures, so any small true effect lies below what current instruments can detect.`,
  ]),
  mix([
    T`For coffee, the literature is mixed. A previous MR study with fewer instruments reported no causal coffee–AF association [5]; observational, acute-exposure randomized and recent genetic evidence points to no harm [4,6,27,28]. Our nominal IVW association does not overturn that consensus: although it survived false-discovery-rate correction, it was heterogeneous, was not robust to sensitivity estimators, and vanished once BMI was modelled jointly. Colocalization reinforced this reading: the coffee signal had no AF counterpart at the CYP1A1–CYP1A2 and AHR caffeine-metabolism loci (PP.H4 ≤ 0.008) but shared a causal variant with AF at the adiposity locus FTO (PP.H4 = 0.894; replicated at 0.906), and caffeine-metabolism-locus instruments were null in both European sources while the remainder carried the entire signal—consistent with pleiotropy through adiposity rather than a caffeine-specific mechanism. The most defensible reading is that habitual coffee intake has no established causal effect on AF in either direction. This reading aligns with the 2026 American Heart Association statement that habitual caffeinated coffee does not increase AF risk and left caffeine-metabolism effect modification unresolved [7]; our locus-level analyses speak directly to that gap.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Potential mechanisms and the role of adiposity`),
  mix([
    T`The BMI positive control (OR 1.47 per SD) reaffirms adiposity as a central causal determinant of AF and offers a parsimonious explanation for the observational literature: habitual SSB intake promotes weight gain, and the resulting adiposity—rather than the beverage per se—increases AF risk via atrial remodelling, epicardial fat, hypertension and sleep apnoea. Two-step mediation MR quantified this pathway: the BMI-mediated indirect effect accounted for the entire nominal coffee association (Table S16). Strong colocalization of the coffee signal with AF at FTO, and its absence at caffeine-metabolism loci, provides locus-level corroboration, and the positive genetic correlations of SSB and ASB intake with AF (rg = 0.24–0.31, q < 0.05) alongside null MR estimates exemplify the distinction: sweetened-drink intake shares heritable architecture with AF without a detectable causal beverage effect. This distinction matters clinically, shifting preventive emphasis towards weight management and dietary pattern [1].`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Clinical and public-health implications`),
  mix([
    T`Current genetic evidence does not support sweetened beverage intake as a direct causal determinant of AF; within the limits of instruments proxying sweetening behaviour rather than milk tea itself, it offers no direct evidence implicating milk-tea-type beverages in AF aetiology, but cannot exclude effects of the magnitude reported observationally. At the trigger level, the one randomized trial of acute coffee exposure likewise found no excess of atrial arrhythmias [27]. This conclusion is not a verdict of safety: the instruments proxy intake categories rather than the acute sugar–caffeine bolus of milk tea, and the cardiometabolic case for limiting added sugar rests on evidence far beyond AF. For young East Asian consumers, the AF-prevention message remains the established one: healthy weight, limited alcohol, cardiometabolic risk control [1]. Prospective studies measuring milk tea intake directly in East Asian cohorts remain a priority.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Strengths and limitations`),
  mix([
    T`Strengths include a causal-inference design with multiple complementary estimators, use of the largest available European-ancestry AF GWAS, prespecified multiplicity control, multivariable modelling and locus-level colocalization, a positive lifestyle control validating analytical sensitivity, and cross-ancestry replication. Several limitations merit emphasis. First, no GWAS measures milk tea itself; our component-proxy strategy assumes that instrumented sweetening behaviour for tea/coffee approximates the exposure of interest and misses the acute sugar–caffeine bolus of one serving. Second, instruments for the five beverage sub-phenotypes were selected at a relaxed threshold (P < 5 × 10⁻⁶) because few variants reached genome-wide significance; although all F statistics exceeded 20, this weakens protection against winner's curse, and exposure GWAS sample sizes limit power (Table S12): the null estimates exclude moderate-to-large but not small effects. Third, exposure phenotypes are ordinal single-questionnaire intake measures, so estimates scale per intake category rather than per gram or millilitre. Fourth, the instruments derive from European-ancestry adults, whereas the target consumers are young East Asians; the BioBank Japan replication is therefore subject to cross-ancestry linkage-disequilibrium and allele-frequency mismatch, and its wide confidence intervals make it supportive context rather than definitive replication; the early-onset AF analysis addressed age but was underpowered to exclude moderate effects [29]. Fifth, AFGen 2018 shares cohorts with Nielsen 2018 and serves only as a sensitivity source. Sixth, UK Biobank participants contribute to both the exposure GWAS and the Nielsen 2018 outcome meta-analysis, so sample overlap could bias estimates; with F statistics of 21–79 this bias should be minimal, the coffee association was stronger in non-overlapping FinnGen R13 (OR 1.58 vs 1.26), and the cross-trait LDSC intercept for coffee × Nielsen 2018 (−0.011, SE 0.008) was indistinguishable from zero, whereas it readily detected the known coffee–tea GWAS overlap (−0.318, SE 0.011). Seventh, AF ascertainment from diagnostic codes under-detects paroxysmal AF, and the FinnGen I9_AF endpoint pools AF with atrial flutter (ICD-10 I48), so FinnGen-specific estimates are not AF-exclusive. Eighth, MR estimates lifelong average exposure and addresses neither acute triggering nor post-ablation recurrence. Finally, mediation MR—conditional on a robust total effect—was performed as a mechanistic probe under the usual product-method assumptions; the negative outcome control was imperfect (atrioventricular block shares adiposity-mediated aetiology with AF), so instrument AF-specificity cannot be formally established; the NHANES triangulation is deferred; public-use NHANES in any case lacks validated AF ascertainment (no AF-specific questionnaire item and no routine electrocardiography since NHANES III), so such triangulation would be uninformative by design.`,
  ]),
  h2(T`Conclusions`),
  mix([
    T`Genetically proxied consumption of sweetened beverages—including the sugar component of tea- and coffee-based drinks—was not causally associated with atrial fibrillation in European or East Asian populations, and a positive lifestyle control confirmed that the design detects established causal lifestyle effects. The modest observational association between sweetened beverages and incident AF is more plausibly explained by residual confounding, including adiposity, than by a direct causal effect of beverage sugar. Prevention efforts for AF are better directed at established cardiometabolic risk factors; direct investigation of milk tea consumption in prospective East Asian cohorts is needed to close the remaining evidence gap.`,
  ])
);

